WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:01.750 align:start position:0%
 
it's not my job to tell  the true story of prince charles that's  not what we're doing at all the crown  can't ever be factually perfect but it  sure is an emotional rollercoaster  especially for the actors involved while  we hope they will do season 5 justice  today we are reflecting on the moments  that they struggled the most to film  number one sometimes beginning is the  hardest part i'm imelda staunton and i'm  currently on the set of the crown while  season five was just in its beginning  stages imelda was feeling just a bit  intimidated by the talent that came  before her i'm delighted to be here  inheriting the role of queen elizabeth  from two outstanding actresses but  taking on a role of such grandeur with  so much love behind its stars imelda was  feeling like it was all too much even  while shooting the promovid just wait  until she gets deeper into the story  hopefully i look calm collected and  capable my stomach meanwhile is doing  somersaults who are you referring to  camilla why would i care about her  because i care about her  number two and sometimes endings are the  hardest part emma and josh really  struggled with an argument in the last  episode the scene was the first time  they lost their temper with each other  which signified the end of their  relationship she shared it was just so  difficult to get to that place after  becoming so close on set josh and i  especially because the two of them had  never explored that dynamic between the  characters before but josh admitted when  they are angry and hating each other as  soon as they call cut we're laughing and  playing games when the prince of wales  told me he was going away for six weeks  and not taking you  i said that's not very nice  number three the object of conflict  being present in the room changes the  atmosphere i wanted just to play around  a little bit before we actually came to  shoot the scene one way to get your  actors duking it out over a man is to  put that man in rehearsal suddenly the  stakes are through the roof especially  when that man is prince charles i had  him sitting in the middle of the table  between both emma and emeralds he gave a  very interesting direction to them both  only one of you can put your hand on his  emerald got there first with her hand  and it made it super uncomfortable for  emma even though we weren't explicitly  doing it we were battling it out over  him  number four next to the heart is the  breath emmacorin was struggling  breathing because it was like my first  week i actually didn't want to make a  big thing of anything her breathing got  so intense but they were in the middle  of shooting a scene so i just remember  feeling like oh my god i can't breathe  but then they were still rolling luckily  prioritizing her health won out in the  end and then eventually had to be like  please someone help me number five  eating disorders are no joke and emma  was adamant she would get it right emma  corrin definitely did her research on  princess diana she asked if they could  expand the script to make sure the  situations weren't isolated with no  context from all her research and  talking with people who'd experienced it  she said it is all-consuming it is  something that your day ends up  revolving around and it obviously has a  huge strain on you mentally and  emotionally i felt that in order to do  the whole of her story justice i had to  include that i felt a lot for diana this  wedding can't go ahead it'll be a  disaster for everyone  number six hide and seek is an effective  acting challenge we don't film things  chronologically asthma wasn't the only  thing working on emma's physical state  turns out a lost phone can do a lot to  get someone into character he hid the  phone so in the room there are like  three rooms i filled with flowers and  everything else the director did this  every take this just launched emma into  the urgency that was necessary for the  character  number seven that feel when you have to  be cruel and all you want is to spread  love and hugs josh o'connor was so  intense as prince charles that he scared  emma he admitted to feeling just  downright awful about the situation  saying i tend to keep myself to myself  especially on days when it's that kind  of emotional scene he admitted that the  entire time he had to scream in her face  all he wanted to do was give her a hug  and say i'm so sorry  number eight destroying something  beautiful proved to be the wrong choice  how dare these people send us flowers  they have no idea what's going on emma  corrin got so intense playing princess  diana she whipped the flowers around the  room in a frenzy i just like threw these  flowers everywhere and destroyed  effectively the whole room unfortunately  the catharsis was too much i think she  wouldn't let go that much even away from  the public eye there's still the sense  she can't just openly express herself  and it's all the more heartbreaking  number nine the only time not listening  is a good choice in order to hide her  intense emotions a lot of scenes had to  be filmed from the back of olivia  coleman's head but when she was being  filmed from the front the poor woman  couldn't contain her tears upon hearing  the sad things so she needed a  distraction they actually decided to put  an earpiece in her so i just have to try  and tune into the shifting force  number 10 you'd think you'd find energy  in nearly completing a project but no  filming the final scene of season two  matt smith and claire foy were nearing  the end of their time in scotland and  were beaten down smith said filming  wasn't great actually i really found it  difficult by that point we were so  entrenched in making the show we were  just tired it just felt like on the day  we were not landing it and it's  complicated you know there's a lot set  up in there and whether or not prince  philip was faithful smith had to choose  for his character he made the dramatic  choice and with one look foy sparked  panic in smith number 11 choosing to  include this tragedy at all was a daring  move by the creative team but they felt  to honor the story it had to be included  it came with a lot of research and the  opening of old wounds some of the extras  who were invited onto the set of the  aberfan tragedy were there to witness it  the producer said we had a therapist to  help all the people who were recreating  such a horrific scene people who live  there are still traumatized of course  and we found they'd never been offered  help before now we are trying to arrange  more helena bunham carter said of olivia  she's about the most empathetic  individual i know lakes of tears inside  her she can't not cry so she couldn't  listen to me making the speech without  crying and the whole point is that she's  playing the queen who finds it difficult  to express emotion  number 12 goodbyes are never easy  vanessa kirby's last day on set had her  weeping her last scene was on the  buckingham palace set and it was of her  and claire having lunch it was intense  to say goodbye to such a character but  what made it better or worse maybe was  the second cut was called everyone  pulled out disco lights and champagne  music started playing she just bawled  more in typical margaret fashion vanessa  was sobbing and claire was holding it  together completely the same it's always  been between the two of them vanessa  said there's a picture of us and i've  got a completely swollen face from  crying and she just looks completely  fine  number 13. we've all had nights like  this something that helped vanessa kirby  understand margaret was imagining that  she had dark nights of the soul that she  has a darkness to her with something  dangerous lurking just underneath  vanessa kirby said peter had just  written that she does a melancholy dance  but i was like margaret doesn't do just  melancholy further admitting i just  tried to access her pain really  number 14. burn the suit playing the  queen mother in the show really did a  number on victoria hamilton's  self-esteem in season 1. to the point  that she almost burned the bodysuit she  had to wear every day on set every part  of it made her more and more miserable  she'd said every day i had to climb into  that and leave the truck with a large  cup of coffee walking like a farmer oh  why is that they become too emotional i  doubt you'll have that trouble with me  number 15 strong writing leads to great  performances but can also just be so  daunting jillian was feeling incredibly  nervous to film the scenes where  margaret thatcher and queen elizabeth ii  get into a heated fight over apartheid  jillian said those were the scenes i was  most looking forward to and most  dreading at the same time  [Music]  you

